    "case_id": "472",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 40s with hypertension was brought to the emergency department after becoming unresponsive during descent on a commercial flight. He was alert and oriented by emergency medical services evaluation and in the emergency department. He had no recollection of the event, with history notable only for more than usual alcohol use the 2 days prior, including drinking just prior to boarding the plane.\n\n- Alert and oriented- Normal heart rate and blood pressure- Jugular venous pressure less than 5 cm H2O\n\n- ECG findings:  - Normal sinus rhythm with left ventricular hypertrophy by Sokolov-Lyon criteria  - Intra-atrial conduction delay with biphasic P wave in leads III and aVF  - 1-2mm ST-segment elevations in leads V1-V3  - 2-3mm ST depressions in leads V5-V6  - T-wave inversions in leads I, aVL, II, and aVF  - Deep T-wave inversions in leads V4-V6- Laboratory tests:  - Creatinine: 2.0 mg/dL  - Potassium: 4.5 mEq/L- Echocardiogram findings:  - Moderate concentric left ventricular hypertrophy  - Left ventricular outflow tract obstruction with systolic anterior motion of mitral valve  - Resting LVOT gradient: 23 mm Hg  - Valsalva LVOT gradient: 123 mm Hg  - Small left ventricular cavity with systolic midventricular obstruction  - Cavitary obliteration",
